#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175827 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175977 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "6a090caa-4c73-5f90-9cb1-dc7dcdfe9e37" :question/math-academy-id "e-8906" :question/problem "Consider the segment of the line $y = 2x$ that lies between the lines $x = 0$ and $x = 2.$ Calculate the surface area generated when this segment is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x\\sqrt{1 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\sqrt{1+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\sqrt{5}[\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}] \\\\  & =4\\pi \\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175828 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175978 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "6ee942e8-9fb9-5dfb-8f9c-aea1dd6d53ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8907" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $x=y^2$ that lies between $y=0$ and $y=2,$ as shown below. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](images/30/30c2b0000055802166e5bdf8ec3e305d83e65ac960043f66463702952d91e4d6.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x = f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})=2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2})\\sqrt{1+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175843 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175992 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "d0546997-c726-5ec2-ae10-c2373ed8b863" :question/math-academy-id "e-9121" :question/problem "Consider the arc of the curve $y=x^3-3x$ that lies in the fourth quadrant between its $x$-intercepts. What integral gives the area of the surface generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\n![](images/77/777c36689da6f6cbcf4ce92a7c5ba17c000a7c442a972851d9d1cafaa9fb2ba4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y = f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to determine the limits of integration. To do this, we find where the curve intersects the $y$-axis by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we need to integrate from $x=0$ to $x= \\sqrt{3}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-3x)=3{x}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}x\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{1+(3{x}^{2}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}x\\sqrt{9{x}^{4}-18{x}^{2}+10}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
